  'AI25-single-provider.test.mjs', // AI-25: one built-in provider, on purpose — openrouter is the only name the registry knows, the removed `claude`/`gemini` names are refused as unsupported (not as a missing key) with a message naming the one that works, `aiConfig` carries no per-provider models for them, and `.env.example` no longer documents their keys
  'AI24-message-times.test.mjs', // AI-24: the member's stored question carries the time it was ASKED — the turn start recorded before the provider is called, honoured by Mongoose as an explicit `createdAt`, with a deliberately slow fake provider so a persist-time stamp could not pass; the row is also read back from the database, a question is never ordered after its answer, and a failed turn still writes nothing
  'AI23-location-breakdown.test.mjs', // AI-23: the compare-two-locations question answered by ONE lookup — `getLocationBreakdown` takes no arguments (nothing to smuggle), is gated on a read permission, reports every location with its own distinct-product count and on-hand units, keeps empty locations and archived stock visible, counts a product once in the business total however many shelves hold it, excludes another tenant, orders by stock on hand, and describes itself in the words a model reaches for
  'AI22-batched-lookups.test.mjs', // AI-22: the prompt asks for independent lookups in ONE response, a response carrying several calls is executed as one round with every result returned in order, and the Anthropic wire sends N parallel tool results as ONE user message with N tool_result blocks (the canonical shape, instead of relying on the gateway to merge consecutive user turns) — written after the device spent 5 provider calls / 51.6 s on a two-location comparison that needs three lookups
